22. FORCE MAJEURE
===============================================================================

22.1 Definition. Neither party is liable for any failure or delay in
performing its obligations, other than an obligation to pay a sum of money,
where that failure or delay results from an event of force majeure within the
meaning of Article 1218 of the French Civil Code, being an event beyond the
control of the debtor, which could not reasonably have been foreseen at the
time of formation of the contract and whose effects could not be avoided by
appropriate measures.

22.2 Illustrations. Without limiting Section 22.1, the following are deemed
to constitute events of force majeure where they meet the conditions of
Article 1218 of the French Civil Code: failure, interruption or degradation
of telecommunications networks, of hosting, cloud or content delivery
infrastructure, or of a public package registry; the withdrawal, suspension,
deprecation or unilateral modification of a technical framework, of a
framework policy, of a vendor list, of an application store policy or of a
platform requirement by its operator; and any act of a public authority or
supervisory authority preventing performance.

22.3 Effects. The affected party shall notify the other party without undue
delay, describing the event and its anticipated duration, and shall use
reasonable efforts to mitigate its effects. Performance is suspended for the
duration of the impediment. Where the impediment persists for more than sixty
(60) days, either party may terminate this Licence by written notice with
immediate effect, without liability, and Section 10 applies to the
consequences of that termination.


